<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kherlen River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kerulen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kherlen is a river of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, about 1,250 kilometres long, which rises in the Khentii Mountains and flows east into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulun-lake">Hulun Lake</a>. Its valley, with that of the Onon, was the homeland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and an early Mongol royal camp at Avarga lay on its banks. Later it was the heart of the Setsen Khan's lands in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kherlen-river.jpg" alt="Kherlen River" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chinneeb · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kherlen_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After ritually declining three times, Ögedei, whom Genghis Khan had wished to succeed him, was proclaimed Khan at the kurultai of Khödöö Aral on the <b>Kherlen River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-13">13 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The battle between the Eastern Mongols and the Ming dynasty was fought on the banks of the <b>Kherlen River</b> on the Mongolian Plateau.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-23">23 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Mongol clans of the Onon and <b>Kherlen</b> river valleys in north-eastern Mongolia united in the early 12th century under Khabul Khan, an ancestor of Genghis Khan, who is said to have defied the Jin dynasty (1115-1234).</blockquote><a href="/state/khamag-mongol">Khamag Mongol</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the descendants of Geresenje, son of Dayan Khan of the Northern Yuan, the princes of eastern Khalkha along the <b>Kherlen</b> river took the title Setsen Khan in the late 16th century, forming one of the three great divisions of Khalkha alongside the Tüsheet Khanate and Zasagt Khanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/setsen-khanate">Setsen Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
